                <pb facs="0153" n="33" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg33"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">821.51/937: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-05-16T10:00:00-05:00">May 16, 1931—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:11 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>61. Department’s 26, May 14, 1 p.m. … President Olaya admits that a balanced
                    budget was one of the first conditions laid down in the agreement of June 30,
                    and he agreed with the bankers on a balanced budget of $51,000,000 afterwards
                    reduced to $49,000,000 for this year. What the President holds is that he cannot
                    revise his budget month by month in accordance with the monthly receipt of
                    revenues; what the bankers now demand is that he reduce the budget again as the
                    receipts for the first quarter are below their estimates. The President says
                    that the revenues will pick up later on. There are no salary reductions involved
                    in the new reductions demanded, but the discharge of large numbers of employees
                    is involved. The 1930 budget reached over $59,000,000.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
